{"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/screenstates\/accessibility"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"seeAlsoSections":[{"anchor":"Related-Documentation","identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithUIKit"],"title":"Related Documentation"}],"sections":[],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates"]]},"metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"ScreenStates"}],"title":"Accessibility","roleHeading":"Article","role":"article"},"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"major":0,"patch":0},"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Accessibility"},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Everything the default placeholders — and your own custom ones — get for free: VoiceOver, Reduce Motion, Increase Contrast, Dynamic Type, and stable identifiers for XCUITest."}],"kind":"article","primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"type":"heading","text":"Overview","level":2,"anchor":"Overview"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A placeholder is often the "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"only","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" thing on screen for a few hundred milliseconds, or the only thing a user with a slow connection sees for much longer than that — so it’s worth it being as accessible as the real content it stands in for. The default "},{"code":"ScreenStateDefault{Empty,Loading,Error}View","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"UIView","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"s handle all of this out of the box; this article rounds up what they do and how a fully custom placeholder can match them."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"heading","text":"VoiceOver announcements","level":2,"anchor":"VoiceOver-announcements"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Each default placeholder announces its own appearance — "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"AccessibilityNotification.Announcement(_:).post()"},{"type":"text","text":" in SwiftUI, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIAccessibility.post(notification:.announcement, argument:)"},{"type":"text","text":" in UIKit — so a screen settling into "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".empty"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".loading"},{"type":"text","text":", or "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".error"},{"type":"text","text":" is heard, not just seen."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState\/accessibilityAnnouncement","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" exposes that same wording for any case, so a fully custom placeholder can announce transitions consistently instead of inventing its own text:","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":[".onAppear {","    if let announcement = state.accessibilityAnnouncement {","        AccessibilityNotification.Announcement(announcement).post()","    }","}"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"It’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"nil"},{"type":"text","text":" for "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".data"},{"type":"text","text":" — your own content is what VoiceOver should read at that point — and, for "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".empty"},{"type":"text","text":", it’s the default “Nothing Here” text; announce your own custom title directly instead if your placeholder shows one, so VoiceOver matches what’s on screen. See "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithUIKit","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" for the full examples."}]},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Reduce Motion","anchor":"Reduce-Motion"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The default Loading placeholder’s ring spins via a repeating rotation animation — purely decorative, and exactly the kind of large, continuous motion Reduce Motion exists to suppress. "},{"code":"ScreenStateDefaultLoadingView","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" reads SwiftUI’s "},{"code":"accessibilityReduceMotion","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" environment value; "},{"code":"ScreenStateDefaultLoadingUIView","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" reads "},{"code":"UIAccessibility.isReduceMotionEnabled","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and also observes "},{"code":"UIAccessibility.reduceMotionStatusDidChangeNotification","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", so an already-visible spinner responds immediately if the setting changes while on screen, not just on the next appearance. Either way, the ring stays static — still colorful, just not spinning — instead of disabling itself entirely, since a static ring still communicates “loading” on its own."}]},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Increase Contrast","anchor":"Increase-Contrast"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"All three default placeholders’ gradient icons\/ring respect Increase Contrast — SwiftUI’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"colorSchemeContrast"},{"type":"text","text":" environment value, UIKit’s "},{"code":"UIAccessibility.isDarkerSystemColorsEnabled","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" (observed live the same way Reduce Motion is). A multi-stop gradient can dip below an acceptable contrast ratio in places a single flat color never does, so when Increase Contrast is on, every gradient is swapped for a solid "},{"code":".label","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":".primary","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" tint instead."}]},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Dynamic Type","anchor":"Dynamic-Type"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The default Empty\/Error placeholders’ text scales with Dynamic Type: SwiftUI’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ErrorView"},{"text":" get this for free from ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ContentUnavailableView"},{"text":"’s own text styles, and ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefaultEmptyUIView"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ErrorUIView"},{"text":"’s labels use ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIFont.preferredFont(forTextStyle: .body, compatibleWith:)"},{"text":". Both are covered up to the largest accessibility size by snapshot tests, so a regression that silently pins the text to a fixed size would be caught. The default Loading placeholder has no text at all — its ring is a fixed-size shape, unaffected by Dynamic Type by design.","type":"text"}]},{"level":2,"type":"heading","anchor":"Identifiers-for-XCUITest","text":"Identifiers for XCUITest"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Every default placeholder exposes a stable ","type":"text"},{"code":"accessibilityIdentifier","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", independent of any localized or custom text, so a UI test can assert which state is showing without string-matching:","type":"text"}]},{"rows":[[[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Identifier"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Shown on","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"screenStates.loading","type":"codeVoice"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultLoadingView","type":"reference"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultLoadingUIView","type":"reference"}],"type":"paragraph"}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"screenStates.empty"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultEmptyUIView","isActive":true,"type":"reference"}],"type":"paragraph"}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"screenStates.error","type":"codeVoice"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultErrorView","type":"reference"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultErrorUIView","type":"reference"}],"type":"paragraph"}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"screenStates.error.retryButton","type":"codeVoice"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The Retry button, when "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"onRetry"},{"type":"text","text":" is given"}],"type":"paragraph"}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"screenStates.error.secondaryActionButton"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The secondary action button, when ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"secondaryAction"},{"text":" is given","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]]],"type":"table","header":"row"}]}],"references":{"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/ScreenState/accessibilityAnnouncement":{"title":"accessibilityAnnouncement","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"var"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"accessibilityAnnouncement"},{"kind":"text","text":": "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"String","preciseIdentifier":"s:SS"},{"ki